 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S444-020\",\n \"clauses\": {\n  \"represent\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F17\", \"template\": \"Shade {fraction_str} of the shape.\", \"reasoning\": \"F17 fills fraction_str from F08, which is a unit fraction (numerator domain is the single member 1; denominator from 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8), so the shading task can address representing unit fractions. The same slot also admits F20 (unit) and F21 (non-unit, named in failing_families).\"},\n  \"identify\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"What fraction does the {name} show?\", \"reasoning\": \"F11 asks the learner to name the fraction a model shows. Its keyed answer is F08, a unit fraction, so the task identifies unit fractions.\"},\n  \"unit_fractions\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F08\", \"template\": \"{num}/{den}\", \"reasoning\": \"The num domain has the single member 1, so every F08 keyed answer is a unit fraction.\"},\n  \"denominators_2_3_4_5_6_8\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F08\", \"template\": \"{num}/{den}\", \"reasoning\": \"The den domain is the grade denom_pool, exactly 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, which matches the clause.\"}\n },\n \"failing_families\": [\"F15\", \"F16\", \"F17\", \"F21\"],\n \"failing_templates\": [\"{numer}/{denom}\", \"{unshaded}/{denom}\", \"Shade {fraction_str} of the shape.\"],\n \"overall\": \"FAIL\",\n \"reasoning\": \"F21 ({unshaded}/{denom}) is a keyed answer (correct_answer) and is nested in F17 (Shade {fraction_str} of the shape.). Its unshaded slot is a computed number derived from numerator and denominator, and for any denominator from 3 to 8 it is greater than 1, so F17 can ask the learner to shade a non-unit fraction. The lc's competency text names only unit fractions, so this is content outside the competency's scope. F15 ({numer}/{denom}) appears in format_data.mcq_options. Its numer slot has a repeats-numerator domain printing only 1, and its denom slot has a repeats-denominator domain printing 2 to 6 and 8. Those domains alone can print the same fraction as the F08 keyed answer on the same item, so a distractor can duplicate the key. F16 ({unshaded}/{denom}) appears in distractors and mcq options. Its unshaded value is a computed difference from the answer's numerator and denominator (the reason is arithmetic). At denominator 2 that difference is 1, which would print the same fraction as the keyed unit fraction at denominator 2. The packet does not show the generator's exclusion rule, so this collision is inferred from the domain and the template name.\"}",
